\paragraph{Proposition.}
The original orbit representatives at positions $305$ and $735$ of job
$[155,4]$ do not generate $G$. Their catalogue indices are respectively
$3116$ and $8142$.

\paragraph{Proof.}
Write the actual four matrices of either representative as
$(r_0,r_1,r_2,r_3)$, in the fixed original order, and put
$R=\Gen{r_1,r_2,r_3}$. The supplied certificate has $756$ orbit lifts
$T_i\in R$ and a subgroup $S\leq R$ of order $16$. Every lift and every
element of $S$ is expressed by a word in these three actual generators.
The separate checker verifies distinct orbit vectors, distinct full
stabilizer matrices, complete stabilizer closure, and every one of the
$3\cdot756=2268$ full matrix equations
\[
 T_i r_j=S_sT_t\qquad(j=1,2,3).
\]
Consequently $E=\bigcup_i ST_i$ is contained in $R$, contains the identity,
and is closed under right multiplication by all three generators. These
generators are involutions, so $E=R$. The distinct orbit vectors distinguish
the cosets, and distinct stabilizer matrices distinguish their members.
Thus
\[
 |R|=756\cdot16=12096.
\]
The same supplied-only check verifies $r_0\in R$ by comparing the entire
matrix with the certified coset representatives. It follows that
\[
 \Gen{r_0,r_1,r_2,r_3}=R,\qquad
 12096<11^6<|G|.
\]
Faithfulness transfers these equalities to the intended original marking.
Conjugation by the accepted original transporters preserves nongeneration,
so the statement applies to the recorded original orbit positions.
\hfill$\square$

